%erg 0.1
Title: Forbid local edits to the shipped tickets/AGENTS.md
Created: 2026-09-10
Author: HDMX-coding-agent
Blocked-by: 0278

--- log ---
2026-09-10T06:53Z HDMX-coding-agent created

2026-09-10T06:57Z note reframed after the 11-repo sweep: one editing instance, not widespread drift; author directs a ban, escape hatch is the on-demand channel; 0278 is a prerequisite
--- body ---
## Context

git-erg installs `tickets/AGENTS.md` into every adopter repo — 2 141 bytes as
shipped (`src/go/assets/AGENTS.md`, embedded and mapped by
`src/go/bootstrap_assets.go`). Adopters then wire it into the agent's *resident*
context: the harness convention is a bare `@tickets/AGENTS.md` line in the
project `CLAUDE.md`, and `erg install --inject-agents` writes a pointer into the
root `AGENTS.md`. Every byte of that file is re-read at the start of every
session, in every session of every adopter repo. Nothing budgets it: the asset
machinery is hash-based and asks one question — pristine or locally edited
(`manifest.go`, `isCleanUpgrade`) — and size is never consulted.

The 11-repo sweep of 2026-09-10 (tabulated in 0278) settles the scale of the
problem, and it is smaller than it looked: **exactly one repo has ever edited
this file.** ImperialDragonHarness stands at 8 005 bytes, 3.7x baseline, roughly
2 000 tokens paid at every session start. Every other adopter is byte-for-byte
a shipped release — six current, five frozen on an older one.

So this is not a widespread drift to be policed. It is one repo's accretion, and
the interesting fact about it is *what* accreted: optimistic-ID collision
recovery, the per-PR `gh pr view` scan, the cross-PR CI gate, artifacts versus
decision records. Almost none of that is harness-specific. It is generic git-erg
lore that had nowhere else to live, so it went into the only file the agent was
guaranteed to read.

## The decision (author, 2026-09-10)

Local editing of the shipped `tickets/AGENTS.md` is to be **forbidden**, not
merely budgeted. That is enforceable precisely because the sweep shows a single
instance — but a ban with no escape hatch would destroy the one repo's content,
which is real and worth keeping. The ban therefore comes with somewhere for the
content to go, and the choice of destination is the whole design:

- **Not** upstreamed into `assets/AGENTS.md`. Folding 5 900 bytes into the
  shipped asset makes every adopter pay ~1 500 resident tokens for war stories
  most of them will never hit. That trades one repo's cost for eleven.
- **Into the on-demand channel git-erg already has.** `erg spec` and
  `erg integration` are commands that print documentation when an agent asks
  for it. Long-form collision recovery belongs there, named by a one-line
  pointer from the shipped `AGENTS.md`. Pointer resident, payload on demand —
  the same split the harness applied to its own rules tree on 2026-09-09,
  measured at 31 000 -> 11 300 resident tokens.
- **Adopter-specific lore** (a repo's own CI job names, its local conventions)
  goes in an adopter-owned file the shipped asset points at, never in the
  shipped asset itself.

With the escape hatch in place the ban is cheap to enforce: an unrecognized hash
becomes a hard error rather than a preserved local edit. Note the coupling — a
hard error on an unrecognized hash is only safe once 0278 has populated
`extraHistoricalHashes`, or the five frozen repos all fail instead of upgrading.
**0278 lands first.**

## Relevant files

- `src/go/assets/AGENTS.md` — the shipped baseline, 2 141 bytes; must stay lean
- `src/go/bootstrap_assets.go` — embeds it; maps `tickets/AGENTS.md` -> `assets/AGENTS.md`
- `src/go/manifest.go` — `isCleanUpgrade`: where pristine-vs-edited is decided
  today, and where the ban's verdict changes
- `src/go/install.go` — `--inject-agents`, the flag that makes the file resident
- `src/go/spec.go`, `src/go/integration.go` (or their equivalents) — the
  on-demand channel the displaced content moves into
- `/home/haduong/.claude/tickets/AGENTS.md` — the single specimen; its excess
  content is the migration's input

## Actions

1. Land 0278 first. The ban cannot be enforced while five adopters carry
   unrecognizable-but-legitimate copies.
2. Partition the harness's 8 005-byte copy: what is generic git-erg lore (most
   of it), what is adopter-specific (CI job names, local paths), what is already
   in the shipped baseline.
3. Move the generic long-form material into the `erg integration` / `erg spec`
   output, and leave a one-line pointer in the shipped `AGENTS.md` naming the
   command that prints it. Keep the shipped file's resident cost within its
   current order of magnitude — a lean file is the point, not a side effect.
4. Give adopter-specific lore a declared home the shipped asset points at, so
   the ban never forces a repo to discard local knowledge.
5. Make an unrecognized hash a hard error on `erg check`, with a message that
   names the escape hatch rather than only refusing.
6. Return the harness copy to pristine and verify the lore is still reachable
   from a cold session — the migration is only done when an agent that needs the
   collision recipe can still find it.

## Test

Red step: a fixture repo whose `tickets/AGENTS.md` carries a local edit,
asserting `erg check` fails and names the escape hatch. Against today's code it
passes silently, so watch it fail before the fix.

The positive control matters as much: a fixture at a recognized shipped hash
must stay silent. A check whose all-clear cannot be told from "I could not look"
is not a check — so demonstrate the failing arm firing before trusting the
passing arm's silence.

Migration test: the collision-recovery material is reachable from `erg integration`
(or `erg spec`) output after the move, asserted on content, not on exit code.

## Verification

- [ ] `erg check` fails on a locally-edited `tickets/AGENTS.md`, message names where the content should go
- [ ] `erg check` stays silent on every hash recognized after 0278
- [ ] the shipped `assets/AGENTS.md` has not grown by more than a pointer
- [ ] `erg integration` output contains the optimistic-ID collision recipe
- [ ] the ImperialDragonHarness copy is byte-identical to the shipped asset, and its lore is reachable from the on-demand channel
- [ ] a cold agent session in an adopter repo can still reach the collision recipe

## Invariants

- No adopter loses content. Every byte removed from a local `AGENTS.md` is
  reachable from a command before the removal lands.
- The shipped asset stays lean: this ticket must not become the vehicle that
  makes eleven repos pay the harness's accumulated cost.
- `erg check`'s exit code is unchanged for a pristine corpus.
- 0278 lands first; enforcing before it would fail five legitimate repos.

## Exit criteria

- A locally-edited shipped `AGENTS.md` is refused by `erg check`, with the
  escape hatch named in the message
- The generic collision lore lives in the on-demand channel, pointed at from the
  shipped asset by one line
- Adopter-specific lore has a declared, documented home outside the shipped asset
- The one specimen (ImperialDragonHarness) is back to pristine with nothing lost
- The design decision above is recorded here, so the next agent does not retake it

## Related

- 0278 — the historical-hash table; a hard prerequisite, not a sibling.
